 ### [Dr.Jit Optimizations](11680-dr-jit-optimizations.md)
-Dr.Jit Optimizations are a set of global transformations applied during the tracing phase to improve code generation, specifically addressing the opaque boundary of dynamic dispatch that breaks interprocedural optimizations in backends like LLVM and OptiX. Unlike ML-centric IR and tensorial optimizations which are costly for rendering, Dr.Jit's optimizations are tailored for the large amount of arithmetic in PBDR workloads.
+Dr.Jit Optimizations are a set of global transformations applied during the tracing phase to improve code generation, specifically addressing the opaque boundary of dynamic dispatch that breaks interprocedural optimizations in backends like LLVM and OptiX. Unlike ML-centric IR and tensorial optimizations which are costly for rendering, Dr.Jit's optimizations are tailored for the large amount of arithmetic in PBDR workloads. Choose it when rendering workloads require aggressive arithmetic simplification without the overhead of tensor-centric passes.
 
 ### [Dr.Jit Tracing and Compilation](11682-dr-jit-tracing-and-compilation.md)
-Dr.Jit Tracing and Compilation is a just-in-time (JIT) system that converts traced Python-like computation graphs into optimized kernels for CPUs and GPUs. It solves the problem of high compilation overhead and poor runtime performance in dynamic dispatch scenarios common in differentiable rendering. It sets itself apart from alternatives like JAX/XLA by generating indirect calls to separate subroutines rather than merging all functions into a single large kernel body or committing loop state to memory at every iteration, thereby reducing backend compilation time and register allocation costs.
+Dr.Jit Tracing and Compilation is a just-in-time (JIT) system that converts traced Python-like computation graphs into optimized kernels for CPUs and GPUs. It solves the problem of high compilation overhead and poor runtime performance in dynamic dispatch scenarios common in differentiable rendering. It sets itself apart from alternatives like JAX/XLA by generating indirect calls to separate subroutines rather than merging all functions into a single large kernel body or committing loop state to memory at every iteration, thereby reducing backend compilation time and register allocation costs. Choose it when control flow preservation is critical to avoid backend compilation bottlenecks.
 
